{"type":"new","contents":"/-\nInfoGeometry/OperatorAlgebra/ChiralPolarization.lean\n\nCircular polarization and defect predicates.\n\nThis module keeps the ambient algebra/module fixed. Nilpotents, zero divisors,\nand chiral sectors are predicates or projector data inside that ambient object;\nthey are not modeled as replacement ambient types.\n\nThe circular chiral basis is generated by an involution `χ² = 1`, morally\n`χ = iK` after scalar complexification of a real phase operator `K² = -1`.\n-/\n\nimport Mathlib\n\nnoncomputable section\n\nnamespace InfoGeometry.OperatorAlgebra\n\n/-! ## 1. Krein isotropic cone as carrier geometry -/\n\n/--\nA Krein fundamental symmetry or metric operator on the carrier.\n\nThe compatibility laws remain proof-carrying at this layer; concrete Krein\nmodels can supply self-adjointness, involutivity, or stronger analytic\nproperties later.\n-/\nstructure KreinMetricDatum\n    (H : Type*) [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H] where\n  eta : H →L[ℝ] H\n  eta_self_adjoint : Prop\n  eta_involutive : Prop\n\n/-- Krein bilinear form associated to `eta`. -/\ndef kreinForm\n    {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]\n    (K : KreinMetricDatum H)\n    (v w : H) : ℝ :=\n  inner (𝕜 := ℝ) v (K.eta w)\n\n/--\nThe isotropic cone is the nonzero null locus of the Krein quadratic form.\n\nThis is the geometric primitive; algebraic zero-divisor or nilpotent defects\nare represented detection loci related to it by bridge witnesses.\n-/\ndef IsIsotropicVector\n    {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]\n    (K : KreinMetricDatum H)\n    (v : H) : Prop :=\n  v ≠ 0 ∧ kreinForm K v v = 0\n\n/--\nProjective ray equivalence by nonzero real scalar rescaling.\n-/\ndef SameProjectiveRay\n    {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [NormedSpace ℝ H]\n    (v w : H) : Prop :=\n  ∃ lam : ℝ, lam ≠ 0 ∧ w = lam • v\n\n/-! ## 2. Defect predicates inside a fixed ambient algebra -/\n\n/--\nA nilpotent element in an ambient power-with-zero structure.\n\nThis repository-local version requires a nonzero exponent.  Mathlib also has\n`IsNilpotent x := ∃ n, x ^ n = 0`.\n-/\ndef IsNilpotentElement\n    {A : Type*} [Zero A] [Pow A ℕ]\n    (x : A) : Prop :=\n  ∃ n : ℕ, n ≠ 0 ∧ x ^ n = 0\n\n/--\nRepository-local nonzero-exponent nilpotence implies Mathlib nilpotence.\n-/\ntheorem isNilpotent_of_isNilpotentElement\n    {A : Type*} [Zero A] [Pow A ℕ]\n    {x : A}\n    (h : IsNilpotentElement x) :\n    IsNilpotent x := by\n  rcases h with ⟨n, _hn_ne, hn⟩\n  exact ⟨n, hn⟩\n\n/-- A square-zero element. -/\ndef IsSquareZeroElement\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A]\n    (x : A) : Prop :=\n  x * x = 0\n\n/--\nA square-zero element is nilpotent with exponent `2`.\n-/\ntheorem isNilpotentElement_of_squareZero\n    {A : Type*} [MonoidWithZero A]\n    {x : A}\n    (h : x * x = 0) :\n    IsNilpotentElement x := by\n  refine ⟨2, by norm_num, ?_⟩\n  simpa [pow_two] using h\n\n/-- A nonzero square-zero element. -/\ndef IsNonzeroSquareZeroElement\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A]\n    (x : A) : Prop :=\n  x * x = 0 ∧ x ≠ 0\n\n/--\nA left zero divisor: some nonzero element is killed on the right by `x`.\n\nThis is a predicate inside an ambient algebra, not a new ambient type.\n-/\ndef IsLeftZeroDivisor\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A]\n    (x : A) : Prop :=\n  ∃ y : A, y ≠ 0 ∧ x * y = 0\n\n/--\nA right zero divisor: some nonzero element kills `x` on the left.\n\nThis is a predicate inside an ambient algebra, not a new ambient type.\n-/\ndef IsRightZeroDivisor\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A]\n    (x : A) : Prop :=\n  ∃ y : A, y ≠ 0 ∧ y * x = 0\n\n/--\nA one-sided zero divisor in a possibly noncommutative ambient algebra.\n-/\ndef IsOneSidedZeroDivisor\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A]\n    (x : A) : Prop :=\n  IsLeftZeroDivisor x ∨ IsRightZeroDivisor x\n\n/--\nA two-sided zero divisor in a possibly noncommutative ambient algebra.\n\nThis requires both left and right annihilator data. For the weaker\nleft-or-right condition, use `IsOneSidedZeroDivisor`.\n-/\ndef IsTwoSidedZeroDivisor\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A]\n    (x : A) : Prop :=\n  IsLeftZeroDivisor x ∧ IsRightZeroDivisor x\n\n/-- A two-sided zero divisor is, in particular, a one-sided zero divisor. -/\ntheorem IsTwoSidedZeroDivisor.oneSided\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A]\n    {x : A}\n    (h : IsTwoSidedZeroDivisor x) :\n    IsOneSidedZeroDivisor x :=\n  Or.inl h.1\n\n/--\nA minimal division-core-like condition: no nonzero square-zero elements.\n\nThis avoids pretending that quaternions/octonions are ordinary commutative\nintegral domains while still capturing the needed nilpotent obstruction.\n-/\nclass NoSquareZero\n    (A : Type*) [MulZeroClass A] : Prop where\n  eq_zero_of_square_eq_zero :\n    ∀ x : A, x * x = 0 → x = 0\n\n/-- Square-zero elements vanish in a `NoSquareZero` ambient algebra. -/\ntheorem square_zero_forces_zero\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A] [NoSquareZero A]\n    {x : A}\n    (hx : x * x = 0) :\n    x = 0 :=\n  NoSquareZero.eq_zero_of_square_eq_zero x hx\n\n/-- No-square-zero algebras have no nonzero square-zero elements. -/\ntheorem not_nonzero_squareZero_of_noSquareZero\n    {A : Type*} [MulZeroClass A] [NoSquareZero A]\n    (x : A) :\n    ¬ IsNonzeroSquareZeroElement x := by\n  intro hx\n  exact hx.2 (NoSquareZero.eq_zero_of_square_eq_zero x hx.1)\n\n/-- A semiring with no zero divisors has no nonzero square-zero elements. -/\ninstance noSquareZero_of_noZeroDivisors\n    {A : Type*} [Semiring A] [NoZeroDivisors A] :\n    NoSquareZero A where\n  eq_zero_of_square_eq_zero := by\n    intro x hx\n    exact (mul_eq_zero.mp hx).elim id id\n\n/-! ## 2. Circular polarization inside an associative operator algebra -/\n\n/--\nA circular polarization datum inside an associative operator algebra.\n\nThe intended model is `χ = iK`, where `K² = -1`, hence `χ² = 1`.  The\nprojectors are packaged with their laws because, in a general noncommutative\noperator algebra, the precise scalar compatibility belongs to the concrete\nrepresentation layer.\n-/\nstructure CircularPolarization\n    (Op : Type*) [Ring Op] [Module ℝ Op] where\n  /-- The chiral involution, morally `χ = iK`. -/\n  chi : Op\n\n  /-- The involution law. -/\n  chi_sq :\n    chi * chi = 1\n\n  /-- Left/circular-positive projector. -/\n  P_left : Op\n\n  /-- Right/circular-negative projector. -/\n  P_right : Op\n\n  /-- Formula for the left projector. -/\n  P_left_def :\n    P_left = (1 / 2 : ℝ) • ((1 : Op) + chi)\n\n  /-- Formula for the right projector. -/\n  P_right_def :\n    P_right = (1 / 2 : ℝ) • ((1 : Op) - chi)\n\n  /-- Idempotence of the left projector. -/\n  P_left_idem :\n    P_left * P_left = P_left\n\n  /-- Idempotence of the right projector. -/\n  P_right_idem :\n    P_right * P_right = P_right\n\n  /-- Orthogonality. -/\n  P_left_mul_right :\n    P_left * P_right = 0\n\n  /-- Orthogonality in the other order. -/\n  P_right_mul_left :\n    P_right * P_left = 0\n\n  /-- Completeness. -/\n  P_sum :\n    P_left + P_right = 1\n\nnamespace CircularPolarization\n\nvariable {Op : Type*} [Ring Op] [Module ℝ Op]\nvariable (C : CircularPolarization Op)\n\n/-- Re-export left idempotence. -/\n@[simp]\ntheorem left_idempotent :\n    C.P_left * C.P_left = C.P_left :=\n  C.P_left_idem\n\n/-- Re-export right idempotence. -/\n@[simp]\ntheorem right_idempotent :\n    C.P_right * C.P_right = C.P_right :=\n  C.P_right_idem\n\n/-- Re-export left/right disjointness. -/\n@[simp]\ntheorem left_mul_right :\n    C.P_left * C.P_right = 0 :=\n  C.P_left_mul_right\n\n/-- Re-export right/left disjointness. -/\n@[simp]\ntheorem right_mul_left :\n    C.P_right * C.P_left = 0 :=\n  C.P_right_mul_left\n\n/-- Re-export completeness. -/\ntheorem left_add_right :\n    C.P_left + C.P_right = 1 :=\n  C.P_sum\n\nend CircularPolarization\n\n/-! ## 3. Phase-preserving algebra morphisms preserve circular projectors -/\n\n/--\nA real-linear multiplicative map between polarized operator algebras that\npreserves the chiral involution.\n\nThis is the algebra-level version of the naturality statement:\n\n`f χ_A = χ_B`.\n-/\nstructure PhasePreservingAlgebraMap\n    {OpA OpB : Type*}\n    [Ring OpA] [Module ℝ OpA]\n    [Ring OpB] [Module ℝ OpB]\n    (CA : CircularPolarization OpA)\n    (CB : CircularPolarization OpB) where\n  toLinearMap : OpA →ₗ[ℝ] OpB\n\n  map_one :\n    toLinearMap 1 = 1\n\n  map_mul :\n    ∀ x y : OpA,\n      toLinearMap (x * y) = toLinearMap x * toLinearMap y\n\n  map_chi :\n    toLinearMap CA.chi = CB.chi\n\nnamespace PhasePreservingAlgebraMap\n\nvariable {OpA OpB : Type*}\nvariable [Ring OpA] [Module ℝ OpA]\nvariable [Ring OpB] [Module ℝ OpB]\nvariable {CA : CircularPolarization OpA}\nvariable {CB : CircularPolarization OpB}\nvariable (f : PhasePreservingAlgebraMap CA CB)\n\n/-- A phase-preserving algebra map preserves the left circular projector. -/\ntheorem map_P_left :\n    f.toLinearMap CA.P_left = CB.P_left := by\n  rw [CA.P_left_def, CB.P_left_def]\n  simp [f.map_one, f.map_chi]\n\n/-- A phase-preserving algebra map preserves the right circular projector. -/\ntheorem map_P_right :\n    f.toLinearMap CA.P_right = CB.P_right := by\n  rw [CA.P_right_def, CB.P_right_def]\n  simp [f.map_one, f.map_chi]\n\nend PhasePreservingAlgebraMap\n\n/-! ## 4. Module-level circular polarization and naturality -/\n\n/--\nCircular polarization on a real module.\n\nThis is the representation-level form: `χ` is a real-linear involution, and\nthe chiral projectors split the carrier module.\n-/\nstructure ModuleCircularPolarization\n    (X : Type*) [AddCommGroup X] [Module ℝ X] where\n  /-- Chiral involution. -/\n  chi : X →ₗ[ℝ] X\n\n  /-- Involution law. -/\n  chi_sq :\n    chi.comp chi = LinearMap.id\n\n  P_left : X →ₗ[ℝ] X\n  P_right : X →ₗ[ℝ] X\n\n  P_left_def :\n    P_left =\n      (1 / 2 : ℝ) •\n        ((LinearMap.id : X →ₗ[ℝ] X) + chi)\n\n  P_right_def :\n    P_right =\n      (1 / 2 : ℝ) •\n        ((LinearMap.id : X →ₗ[ℝ] X) - chi)\n\n  P_left_idem :\n    P_left.comp P_left = P_left\n\n  P_right_idem :\n    P_right.comp P_right = P_right\n\n  P_left_comp_right :\n    P_left.comp P_right = 0\n\n  P_right_comp_left :\n    P_right.comp P_left = 0\n\n  P_sum :\n    P_left + P_right = LinearMap.id\n\n/--\nA phase-preserving linear map between circularly polarized modules.\n\nThis is the precise categorical content:\n\n`f ∘ χ_X = χ_Y ∘ f`.\n-/\nstructure PhasePreservingLinearMap\n    {X Y : Type*}\n    [AddCommGroup X] [Module ℝ X]\n    [AddCommGroup Y] [Module ℝ Y]\n    (CX : ModuleCircularPolarization X)\n    (CY : ModuleCircularPolarization Y) where\n  toLinearMap : X →ₗ[ℝ] Y\n\n  preserves_chi :\n    toLinearMap.comp CX.chi = CY.chi.comp toLinearMap\n\nnamespace PhasePreservingLinearMap\n\nvariable {X Y : Type*}\nvariable [AddCommGroup X] [Module ℝ X]\nvariable [AddCommGroup Y] [Module ℝ Y]\nvariable {CX : ModuleCircularPolarization X}\nvariable {CY : ModuleCircularPolarization Y}\nvariable (f : PhasePreservingLinearMap CX CY)\n\n/-- Pointwise phase-preservation. -/\ntheorem preserves_chi_apply\n    (x : X) :\n    f.toLinearMap (CX.chi x) =\n      CY.chi (f.toLinearMap x) := by\n  have h :=\n    congrArg\n      (fun L : X →ₗ[ℝ] Y => L x)\n      f.preserves_chi\n  simpa [LinearMap.comp_apply] using h\n\n/--\nA phase-preserving map preserves the left chiral projector:\n\n`f ∘ P_L^X = P_L^Y ∘ f`.\n-/\ntheorem comp_P_left :\n    f.toLinearMap.comp CX.P_left =\n      CY.P_left.comp f.toLinearMap := by\n  ext x\n  rw [CX.P_left_def, CY.P_left_def]\n  simp [LinearMap.comp_apply, f.preserves_chi_apply x]\n\n/--\nA phase-preserving map preserves the right chiral projector:\n\n`f ∘ P_R^X = P_R^Y ∘ f`.\n-/\ntheorem comp_P_right :\n    f.toLinearMap.comp CX.P_right =\n      CY.P_right.comp f.toLinearMap := by\n  ext x\n  rw [CX.P_right_def, CY.P_right_def]\n  simp [LinearMap.comp_apply, f.preserves_chi_apply x]\n\nend PhasePreservingLinearMap\n\nend InfoGeometry.OperatorAlgebra\n","mtime":1778084836607}